// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma
// VWK Process/Thread Subsystem — CFS-like scheduler on the superset TrapFrame.
// Wave R4a+b (PMT-pure) + W36 (priority-weighted vruntime, sys_sched_yield,
// sys_nice, sched_dump).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the R4a+b rewrite of K4b's scheduler. K4b built the CFS
// runqueue (sched_init / sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue / sched_tick /
// sched_yield) and a `schedule(rq, tbl, tf)` that took an arch-specific
// Task-with-nested-TrapFrame and switched into it. R4a+b narrows the
// scheduler's surface to the superset TrapFrame (R2a) ONLY:
//
//   * `schedule(rq, tbl) -> u32`     — picks min-vruntime task, returns its
//                                       index (or 256 if empty). The CALLER
//                                       saves/restores the context.
//   * `sched_save_context(tbl, idx, tf)`    — copies tf.{pc,sp,status} into
//                                              the Task slot's saved-context
//                                              fields (mm_root / fs_root /
//                                              fds — see "Saved-context
//                                              overload" below).
//   * `sched_restore_context(tbl, idx, tf)` — copies the saved-context
//                                              fields back into tf.{pc,sp,
//                                              status}.
//
// The scheduler no longer takes a TrapFrame parameter to schedule(); the
// arch-independent caller (kmain, R4c's syscall dispatcher, K4c's timer
// trampoline) does the save/restore around the schedule() call. This matches
// the R2 Golden Rule: "Do not write arch-conditional logic inside kernel
// transforms." The schedule() transform is purely numeric (it scans
// vruntime values and returns an index); the save/restore transforms are
// purely field-copies (tf.{pc,sp,status} ↔ tbl.{mm_root,fs_root,fds}); no
// arch-conditional code is needed.
//
// The per-arch `arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf)` in
// womb/kernel/arch/{x86_64,aarch64,riscv64}/switch.vuma is the arch-specific
// twin of this file: it does the actual CPU register file load (via the
// `arch_load_context(tf)` extern that the R4a+b switch.vuma files declare).
// In hosted mode both `arch_context_switch` and `arch_load_context` are
// no-ops (the scheduler's save/restore already copied the TrapFrame data;
// the bare-metal asm stub that actually loads registers into the CPU is
// K11's switch.S concern, NOT this wave's).
//
// ── Saved-context overload (pc/sp/status → mm_root/fs_root/fds) ──────────────
// The Task slot's u64 fields `mm_root`, `fs_root`, `fds` are normally used
// for page-table root / VFS root / fd-table address (K4a's task.vuma layout).
// During a context switch, however, those fields are NOT dereferenced — the
// switch path runs in the kernel's own address space with the kernel's own
// VFS root and fd table, so the per-task mm/fs/fds are dormant. R4a+b
// overloads them as the "saved context" slots:
//     tbl.mm_root[idx] ← tf.pc       (saved program counter)
//     tbl.fs_root[idx] ← tf.sp       (saved stack pointer)
//     tbl.fds[idx]     ← tf.status   (saved status register)
//
// This is the "actually better" option from the R4a+b contract. The fields
// are u64 — wide enough to hold any register value on all 3 arches
// (x86_64/aarch64/riscv64 are all 64-bit). The contract's "during context
// switch the mm/fs/fds are not accessed" rationale is the safety argument:
// even though we overwrite the task's REAL mm_root with the saved PC, the
// switch code path doesn't read it back as a page-table root — it reads it
// back as a saved PC. When the task is later scheduled again, the restore
// path puts the saved PC back into the TrapFrame, and the SAME slot is
// re-populated with the REAL page-table root by K4d's fork/exec before the
// task runs again (or by K11's kmain when it boots the first task).
//
// The `next` field is NOT overloaded — it remains the runqueue linked-list
// pointer (K4b's sched_enqueue/sched_dequeue write it). Using `next` for
// saved PC would race with the runqueue logic: sched_enqueue overwrites
// `next` to thread the new tail into the list, which would clobber a saved
// PC. The mm_root/fs_root/fds triplet is safe because the runqueue logic
// NEVER touches them — they're only written by K4d's fork (page-table
// setup) and R4a+b's sched_save_context (the overload).
//
// (The R4a+b contract also mentions a "SIMPLER: next/mm_root/fs_root"
// variant. This file uses the safer mm_root/fs_root/fds variant because it
// doesn't touch the runqueue-critical `next` field. The contract leaves
// the exact field assignment open — "Document this clearly." — and this
// file header IS the documentation.)
//
// ── CFS-like scheduling (same as K4b) ───────────────────────────────────────
// The Completely Fair Scheduler (Linux's default since 2.6.23) gives every
// runnable task a fair share of CPU time by tracking each task's "virtual
// runtime" (vruntime) — a nanosecond-precision counter of CPU time the
// task has consumed, weighted by its priority. The scheduler picks the
// task with the LOWEST vruntime (the one that has had the least CPU time
// recently). R4a+b keeps K4b's simplified CFS:
//   * The runqueue is a singly linked list of Task indices (0..255),
//     threaded through Task.next. The list head/tail live in the Runqueue
//     struct. Tail-insertion (sched_enqueue) gives FIFO ordering for tasks
//     with equal vruntimes — same as Linux's rbtree leftmost-with-tie-
//     break-on-insertion-order behavior.
//   * sched_dequeue walks the entire runqueue, finds the task with min
//     vruntime, removes it from the list, and returns its index. (Linux
//     uses an rbtree for O(log n) min-vruntime lookup; K4b/R4a+b uses a
//     linear scan for O(n) — simpler and adequate for the 256-slot
//     ProcessTable.)
//   * schedule(rq, tbl) is just sched_dequeue — the CFS "pick next" step.
//     R4a+b REMOVES the TrapFrame parameter that K4b's schedule() had.
//   * sched_tick(rq, tbl, task_idx, delta) bumps the current task's vruntime
//     by delta (called by the timer IRQ handler on every tick), then runs
//     the CFS preemption check (W11S2): walks the runqueue linked list to
//     find the min vruntime among READY tasks, and if current.vruntime
//     - min > PREEMPT_THRESHOLD (1000000) sets rq.need_resched = 1.
//   * sched_yield(rq, tbl, current) re-enqueues the current task and
//     picks the next one (cooperative yield).
//
// ── Task states used by the scheduler ───────────────────────────────────────
// Same values as K4a's task.vuma:
//     RUNNING = 1  — currently running on a CPU (set by sched_dequeue)
//     READY   = 2  — runnable, waiting for a CPU (set by sched_enqueue)
// state == 0 means the slot is FREE (unused — not on any runqueue).
// sched_enqueue writes state = 2 (READY); sched_dequeue writes state = 1
// (RUNNING) on the picked task before returning it.
//
// ── Runqueue layout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   head:         u32 — index of the first task in the runqueue, or 256 = empty
//   tail:         u32 — index of the last task in the runqueue, or 256 = empty
//   count:        u32 — number of tasks currently in the runqueue (0..256)
//   need_resched: u32 — W11S2 preemption flag (0 = no resched, 1 = sched_tick
//                        observed vruntime drift > PREEMPT_THRESHOLD and the
//                        timer-IRQ handler should call schedule()).
// head/tail are u32 (not u64) to match ProcessTable's index type —
// pt_set_next takes idx: u32, and rq.head / rq.tail are passed directly
// to pt_set_next as the prev-of-new-tail argument. 256 is the "empty"
// sentinel: it's outside the valid 0..255 Task-index range, so it can't
// collide with a real Task slot. Same sentinel value K4a's task_alloc
// returns when the table is full.
//
// The runqueue's `next` linkage lives in ProcessTable.nexts (not in
// Runqueue itself) — K4a's Task layout already has a `next` field for
// exactly this purpose. The sentinel value used in Task.next is also 256
// (NOT 0 — K4a's comment says "0 = end of list" but K4b standardizes on
// 256 for consistency with the rest of the scheduler's empty-sentinel
// convention; both work because Task 0 is a real task, but 256 is never
// a real task).
//
// ── ProcessTable re-declaration ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the
// ProcessTable layout byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. ProcessTable
// has 10 fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + a count u32) totaling
// 14340 bytes — same as K4a.
//
// This file re-declares the 12 ProcessTable helpers it needs:
//   pt_get_state / pt_set_state           (u8 direct-index)
//   pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime     (u64 8-byte LE)
//   pt_get_next / pt_set_next             (u64 8-byte LE — runqueue linkage)
//   pt_get_mm_root / pt_set_mm_root       (u64 8-byte LE — saved PC overload)
//   pt_get_fs_root / pt_set_fs_root       (u64 8-byte LE — saved SP overload)
//   pt_get_fds / pt_set_fds               (u64 8-byte LE — saved status overload)
// These 12 helpers are byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma. The remaining 6
// (pid/ppid get/set, prio get/set) are NOT re-declared here because the
// scheduler doesn't use them — K4d/K4e will re-declare those when needed.
//
// ── Superset TrapFrame re-declaration ───────────────────────────────────────
// R2a created the canonical 576-byte superset TrapFrame in
// womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma. R2b/R2c/R2d re-declared it byte-
// identically in the 3 arch trap_trampoline.vuma files. R4a+b re-declares
// it here byte-identically (same field names, types, order, _pad size) so
// the scheduler can read/write tf.{pc,sp,status} without needing `import`.
// The byte-identical-redeclaration invariant (K2c extended to layouts in
// K3d) applies: this file's TrapFrame MUST have the same field offsets
// as R2a's trap_frame.vuma.
//
// ── Why init-style? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The R4a+b main() allocates `rq = state_new(Runqueue)`, `tbl =
// state_new(ProcessTable)`, and `tf = state_new(TrapFrame)`, and passes
// them by reference to sched_init / sched_enqueue / schedule /
// sched_save_context / sched_restore_context. This is the canonical PMT
// pattern (K2a's pmm_init, K3d's trap_frame_init, K4a's pt_init — see
// pmm.vuma's "Why init-style?" header for the codegen rationale: the
// current codegen does NOT propagate State-typedness through function
// return values, so `fn sched_new() -> State<Runqueue>` would produce
// broken field-access code in callers). All scheduler.vuma functions
// take `rq: State<Runqueue>`, `tbl: State<ProcessTable>`, and/or
// `tf: State<TrapFrame>` by regular VUMA-fn borrow (NOT extern #[borrow]
// — VUMA fn calls don't need the attribute; only extern "C" calls do).
//
// State<T> params: per the contract's "Keep State<T> params to 1-2 per
// function" rule, no function takes more than 2 State<T> params:
//   sched_init(rq)                          — 1 param
//   sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, task_idx)        — 2 params + u32
//   sched_dequeue(rq, tbl)                  — 2 params
//   schedule(rq, tbl)                       — 2 params
//   sched_tick(rq, tbl, task_idx, delta)    — 2 params + u32 + u64 (W11S2:
//                                              rq added for need_resched)
//   sched_yield(rq, tbl, current)           — 2 params + u32
//   sched_save_context(tbl, task_idx, tf)   — 2 params + u32
//   sched_restore_context(tbl, task_idx, tf) — 2 params + u32
//   sched_check_resched(rq)                 — 1 param (W11S2)
//   sched_clear_resched(rq)                 — 1 param (W11S2)
// Same discipline as K2a's pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, order) (2 State params +
// 1 small arg), K3d's trap_frame_save(tf) (1 State param).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. Runqueue,
// ProcessTable, and TrapFrame are all ___pmt_buffer-backed State<T>'s
// allocated by the CALLER via `state_new(...)` and passed by reference.
// No externs are declared in this file — the per-arch context-switch
// externs (arch_load_context) live in the 3 switch.vuma files (R4a+b).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale). The
// constants:
//     256   = "empty" sentinel for runqueue head/tail (also "no next"
//             in Task.next, also "table full" from task_alloc)
//     8     = bytes per u64 field (loop bound for vruntime/next/
//             mm_root/fs_root/fds pack/unpack)
//     255   = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     24    = _pad array size in TrapFrame (byte-zeroing loop bound)
//     2     = READY state value (set by sched_enqueue on the new task)
//     1     = RUNNING state value (set by sched_dequeue on the picked task)
//     0     = FREE state value (set by tf_init and state_new's zero-fill)
//     1000000 = PREEMPT_THRESHOLD (W11S2): vruntime-drift gap in ns above
//             which sched_tick sets rq.need_resched = 1. Decimal (not
//             0xF4240) per the decimal-constants rule.
//     1     = need_resched "request preemption" value (W11S2)
//   Self-test constants: 100, 50, 4096, 2000000 — all decimal.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares TrapFrame + tf_init +
// tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr byte-identically to R2a's trap_frame.vuma, and
// ProcessTable + 12 helpers byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. The 3
// arch switch.vuma files (R4a+b) re-declare TrapFrame + helpers byte-
// identically too — same pattern as R2b/R2c/R2d re-declaring the
// superset TrapFrame in each arch's trap_trampoline.vuma.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma \
//         /tmp/scheduler.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/scheduler.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the self-test enqueues 2 tasks with
// different vruntimes, calls schedule() and verifies the min-vruntime
// task is picked, exercises sched_save_context + sched_restore_context
// round-trip through tf.pc = 4096, and — W11S2 extension — verifies
// sched_tick's preemption check sets rq.need_resched = 1 when the
// current vruntime drifts > 1000000 above the runqueue min, then
// exercises sched_check_resched + sched_clear_resched. W36 extension:
// verifies priority-weighted vruntime (prio=7 → delta/8 decay), sys_nice
// adjust + clamp to 0..139, sys_sched_yield picks min-vruntime READY
// task and updates PerCpu.current_task, and sched_dump prints all 5
// non-FREE tasks to stdout).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Canonical superset TrapFrame (576 bytes total) — re-declared byte-
// identically to R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma. See R2a's header
// for the field semantics (gpr 32×u64 LE-packed in 256 bytes; pc/status/
// vector/error_code/sp are 5 scalar u64 fields; fpu is 32×u64 LE-packed
// in 256 bytes; _pad is 24 alignment bytes).
layout TrapFrame = {
    gpr: [u8; 256],
    pc: u64,
    status: u64,
    vector: u64,
    error_code: u64,
    sp: u64,
    fpu: [u8; 256],
    _pad: [u8; 24],
}

// Runqueue: head/tail are Task indices (0..255) or 256 (empty). count is
// the number of tasks currently in the runqueue (0..256). The next-link
// field lives in ProcessTable.nexts (not here).
//
// need_resched is the preemption flag set by sched_tick (W11S2) when the
// current task's vruntime has drifted more than PREEMPT_THRESHOLD
// (1000000 ns) above the minimum vruntime among the READY tasks in the
// runqueue. The timer-IRQ handler (R4c) reads it via sched_check_resched
// to decide whether to call schedule(), and clears it via
// sched_clear_resched once the preemption has been serviced. Semantically
// equivalent to Linux's TIF_NEED_RESCHED thread flag, but stored on the
// Runqueue (VUMA has no globals — see "Why init-style?" header) rather
// than per-task, because only the currently-running task can be the
// preemptee and the runqueue is the natural per-CPU container.
layout Runqueue = {
    head: u32,
    tail: u32,
    count: u32,
    need_resched: u32,
}

// ProcessTable: re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. 10
// fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340
// bytes. See K4a's task.vuma header "Why parallel flat byte arrays?" for
// the rationale (VUMA has no struct-array support).
layout ProcessTable = {
    pids: [u8; 1024],
    ppids: [u8; 1024],
    states: [u8; 256],
    prios: [u8; 256],
    vruntimes: [u8; 2048],
    mm_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fs_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fds: [u8; 2048],
    nexts: [u8; 2048],
    count: u32,
}

// PerCpu: re-declared byte-identically to K4f's
// womb/kernel/smp/percpu.vuma. W36 (this wave) adds sys_sched_yield which
// needs to read+write `current_task` on the calling CPU's PerCpu block. Only
// the 2 fields sys_sched_yield touches are documented here; the full 6-field
// semantics live in percpu.vuma's header (K4f).
//
// Field semantics (subset relevant to W36):
//   cpu_id        = this CPU's index (0..N-1).
//   current_task  = index of the Task slot currently RUNNING on this CPU
//                   (or 256 = idle). sys_sched_yield reads this to find
//                   "self" and writes it back to the picked "next" task.
//   kernel_stack  = top of this CPU's kernel stack (unused here).
//   idle_task     = index of this CPU's idle task (unused here).
//   rq_head       = runqueue head index (unused here — sys_sched_yield walks
//                   the ProcessTable directly rather than the rq linked list,
//                   so it doesn't need the Runqueue parameter).
//   preempt_count = preemption depth (unused here).
layout PerCpu = {
    cpu_id: u32,
    current_task: u32,
    kernel_stack: u64,
    idle_task: u32,
    rq_head: u32,
    preempt_count: u32,
}

// SchedBuf: 32-byte scratch buffer used by sched_dump's decimal-conversion
// helper (sched_write_u64). 20 bytes is enough for the longest u64 decimal
// (18446744073709551615 = 20 digits); 32 gives headroom. The `data` field
// is at offset 0 so `buf as Address` yields the address of data[0] — the
// natural argument for `write(1, addr, len)`. Same offset-0-buf pattern as
// K12b's KmsgMsg (womb/kernel/panic/kmsg.vuma) and K12a's PanicMsg
// (womb/kernel/panic/panic.vuma).
layout SchedBuf = {
    data: [u8; 32],
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern declarations (NEW in W36 — sched_dump writes to fd 1).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Mirror of womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trampoline.vuma:53 (write only —
// sched_dump is the only extern caller in this file). Pre-registered on
// x86_64 — no __ffi_fallback_stub warning emitted. Byte-identical to the
// write extern in K12a's panic.vuma and K12b's kmsg.vuma.
extern "C" {
    // sys_write (nr 1): write `count` bytes from `buf` to file descriptor
    // `fd`. SysV AMD64: RDI=fd, RSI=buf, RDX=count. Returns byte count or
    // -errno. Used by sched_dump + sched_write_u64 only.
    fn write(fd: i64, buf: Address, count: i64) -> i64;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TrapFrame helpers (re-declared from R2a's trap_frame.vuma, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// tf_init + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr, byte-identical to R2a. The scheduler
// uses tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr for the GPR file (e.g. a future
// sched_save_full_context could save all 32 GPR slots — R4a+b only saves
// the 3 scalar fields pc/sp/status, but the helpers are re-declared here
// so any downstream scheduler function that wants them has them in scope).

// Read the u64 GPR at canonical slot `idx` from tf.gpr. 8-byte LE unpack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Byte-identical to R2a's tf_get_gpr.
fn tf_get_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tf.gpr[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `val` to canonical slot `idx` in tf.gpr. 8-byte LE pack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Byte-identical to R2a's tf_set_gpr.
fn tf_set_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tf.gpr[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Zero every field of a caller-allocated TrapFrame. Called once per
// TrapFrame before the arch-specific trap-to-superset adapter (R2b/R2c/
// R2d) populates the arch-specific fields, OR before the scheduler uses
// it as a fresh save area. Byte-identical to R2a's tf_init.
fn tf_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { tf.gpr[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    let j = 0;
    while j < 256 { tf.fpu[j] = 0; j = j + 1; }
    let k = 0;
    while k < 24 { tf._pad[k] = 0; k = k + 1; }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProcessTable helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 12 of K4a's 18 helpers, byte-identical to task.vuma. The scheduler needs:
//   state (u8 direct-index)
//   vruntime, next, mm_root, fs_root, fds (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack)
// The other 6 helpers (pid/ppid get/set, prio get/set) are NOT re-declared
// here — K4d/K4e will re-declare them when they need them.

// Read the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.states.
fn pt_get_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.states[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.states.
fn pt_set_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.states[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` from
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. 8-byte LE unpack loop (same pattern as K2a's
// pool_get_base, R2a's tf_get_gpr).
fn pt_get_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.vruntimes[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. 8-byte LE pack loop.
fn pt_set_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.vruntimes[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.nexts.
// The scheduler uses Task.next to thread the runqueue linked list.
fn pt_get_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.nexts[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.nexts.
fn pt_set_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.nexts[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `mm_root` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.mm_roots.
// R4a+b overloads this slot as the SAVED PC during context switch — see
// the file header "Saved-context overload" section.
fn pt_get_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.mm_roots[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `mm_root` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.mm_roots. R4a+b sched_save_context writes tf.pc here.
fn pt_set_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.mm_roots[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `fs_root` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.fs_roots.
// R4a+b overloads this slot as the SAVED SP during context switch.
fn pt_get_fs_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.fs_roots[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `fs_root` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.fs_roots. R4a+b sched_save_context writes tf.sp here.
fn pt_set_fs_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.fs_roots[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `fds` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.fds.
// R4a+b overloads this slot as the SAVED STATUS register during context switch.
fn pt_get_fds(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.fds[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `fds` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.fds.
// R4a+b sched_save_context writes tf.status here.
fn pt_set_fds(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.fds[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// W36 additions: prio + pid helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R4a+b deliberately omitted pt_get_prio / pt_set_prio / pt_get_pid from
// the 12 helpers it re-declared, on the grounds that "K4d/K4e will re-
// declare them when they need them." W36 needs them NOW:
//   * sched_tick (W36 priority-weighted vruntime) reads prio.
//   * sys_nice (W36) reads + writes prio.
//   * sched_dump (W36) reads pid, prio, state, vruntime for each non-FREE
//     task.
// Byte-identical to task.vuma's pt_get_prio / pt_set_prio / pt_get_pid.

// Read the u8 `prio` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.prios.
// Direct array index (no pack/unpack — u8 field). Byte-identical to K4a.
fn pt_get_prio(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.prios[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `prio` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.prios.
// Direct array index. Byte-identical to K4a.
fn pt_set_prio(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.prios[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u32 `pid` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.pids.
// 4-byte LE unpack loop (pids is [u8; 1024] = 256 × 4-byte u32 LE slots).
// Byte-identical to K4a's pt_get_pid.
fn pt_get_pid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u32 {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let v: u32 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.pids[off + i] as u32;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Runqueue operations (byte-identical to K4b).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// sched_init: initialize an empty runqueue. Idempotent. Called by kmain
// at boot before the first sched_enqueue. head = tail = 256 (empty
// sentinel), count = 0, need_resched = 0 (no preemption requested until
// sched_tick observes vruntime drift > PREEMPT_THRESHOLD).
fn sched_init(rq: State<Runqueue>) {
    rq.head = 256;
    rq.tail = 256;
    rq.count = 0;
    rq.need_resched = 0;
}

// sched_enqueue: add task `task_idx` to the tail of the runqueue. Sets
// the task's state to READY (2). Tail-insertion gives FIFO ordering for
// tasks with equal vruntimes. Updates Task.next of the old tail to point
// at the new task, and sets the new task's Task.next to 256 (end-of-list).
// Bumps rq.count.
fn sched_enqueue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32) {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        rq.head = task_idx;
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);
    } else {
        pt_set_next(tbl, rq.tail, task_idx);
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);
    }
    rq.count = rq.count + 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, task_idx, 2);
}

// sched_dequeue: remove and return the task with the LOWEST vruntime
// (CFS "pick next" step). Walks the runqueue linked list once to find
// the min-vruntime task (tracking `prev_of_min` so the removal step can
// relink the predecessor), removes it from the list, sets its state to
// RUNNING (1), decrements rq.count, and returns its index.
//
// Returns 256 if the runqueue is empty.
fn sched_dequeue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        return 256;
    }
    let min_idx = rq.head;
    let min_vruntime = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, min_idx);
    let cur = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
    let prev_of_min = 256;
    let prev = min_idx;
    while cur != 256 {
        let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, cur as u32);
        if v < min_vruntime {
            min_idx = cur as u32;
            prev_of_min = prev;
        }
        prev = cur as u32;
        cur = pt_get_next(tbl, cur as u32);
    }
    if min_idx == rq.head {
        rq.head = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx) as u32;
        if rq.head == 256 {
            rq.tail = 256;
        }
    } else {
        let min_next = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
        pt_set_next(tbl, prev_of_min, min_next);
        if min_idx == rq.tail {
            rq.tail = prev_of_min;
        }
    }
    rq.count = rq.count - 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, min_idx, 1);
    return min_idx;
}

// schedule: pick the next task to run. CFS "pick min vruntime". Returns
// the task index, or 256 if no tasks are runnable.
//
// R4a+b CHANGE: schedule no longer takes a TrapFrame parameter. The
// CALLER is responsible for:
//   1. sched_save_context(tbl, prev_idx, prev_tf)  — save prev's PC/SP/status
//   2. next_idx = schedule(rq, tbl)                — pick next
//   3. sched_restore_context(tbl, next_idx, next_tf) — restore next's regs
//   4. arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf)       — actual CPU load (per
//                                                      arch switch.vuma)
// In hosted mode step 4 is a no-op; in bare-metal (K11) it loads next_tf's
// GPRs into the CPU via the arch_load_context extern declared in the per-
// arch switch.vuma file.
fn schedule(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    return sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
}

// sched_tick: bump task `task_idx`'s vruntime by `delta`, then run the
// CFS preemption check (W11S2). Called by the timer-IRQ handler (R4c) on
// every tick — delta is typically the tick interval in nanoseconds.
//
// After bumping vruntime, walks the runqueue linked list (the set of
// READY tasks waiting for a CPU) to find the minimum vruntime among
// them. If `current.vruntime - min_vruntime` exceeds PREEMPT_THRESHOLD
// (1000000 ns — decimal, see the "Decimal-constants discipline" header),
// sets `rq.need_resched = 1` so the timer-IRQ handler knows to call
// schedule() on the next iteration.
//
// The currently-running task is NOT in the runqueue at tick time
// (sched_dequeue removed it when it transitioned RUNNING — see the
// sched_dequeue comment), so the linked-list walk only sees READY tasks
// — exactly the candidates we want to compare against. If the runqueue
// is empty (no other tasks are waiting), `need_resched` is left
// unchanged: there is nothing to preempt TO, so we don't request a
// reschedule (the timer-IRQ handler will keep running the current task).
//
// The `current.vruntime < min_vruntime` case (current task is still the
// most-fair candidate — should not be preempted) is guarded by an
// explicit `if cur_vruntime > min_vruntime` check, because u64
// subtraction would underflow otherwise (VUMA u64 is unsigned wrap; the
// guard avoids relying on wrap semantics for correctness).
//
// State<T> params: 2 (rq + tbl). rq is written (need_resched flag),
// tbl is read (vruntime get/set + linked-list walk). No `*ptr`/`&x`/
// `allocate`/`free` anywhere — pure State<T> field access.
//
// ── W36: priority-weighted vruntime (CFS sched_weight) ──────────────────────
// W36 changes Step 1 from `vruntime += delta` to a priority-weighted
// increment. The CFS paper (Molnar 2007) scales each task's vruntime
// increment by its priority weight so high-prio tasks (large weight) see
// their vruntime grow SLOWLY (they get more CPU before being preempted)
// and low-prio tasks (small weight) see it grow QUICKLY (they get less
// CPU). The canonical CFS formula is:
//     delta_weighted = delta * weight / 1024
// where weight is looked up from a prio→weight table ( sched_prio_to_weight
// in Linux's kernel/sched/sched.h). W36 uses the simplified closed-form
//     weight = 1024 / (prio + 1)
// which gives:
//   prio 0   → weight 1024 → delta_weighted = delta    (no decay — top prio)
//   prio 7   → weight  128 → delta_weighted = delta/8  (1/8 decay)
//   prio 15  → weight   64 → delta_weighted = delta/16
//   prio 31  → weight   32 → delta_weighted = delta/32
//   prio 127 → weight    8 → delta_weighted = delta/128
//   prio 139 → weight    7 → delta_weighted = delta*7/1024  (max decay)
// The `+1` avoids div-by-zero when prio == 0. The 1024 base matches Linux's
// NICE_0_LOAD (the weight of a nice-0 task = 1024 = the unit weight), so
// the math is directly comparable to the upstream CFS scaling.
//
// This is a SIMPLIFICATION of Linux's prio_to_weight[] table (which has
// non-linear entries for nice -20..+19); W36's closed-form gives the same
// monotonic "higher prio → slower vruntime growth" behavior without the
// 40-entry table lookup. K13+ may swap in the real table.
//
// The preemption check (Step 2) is UNCHANGED — it compares the post-bump
// weighted vruntime to the runqueue min, same as W11S2. Because all tasks
// use the same weighting formula, the relative ordering is consistent: a
// high-prio task's vruntime grows slower, so it stays below the min longer,
// so it's preempted less. Exactly CFS's intended behavior.
fn sched_tick(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, delta: u64) {
    // Step 1: bump vruntime by the PRIORITY-WEIGHTED delta (W36).
    //   weight = 1024 / (prio + 1)        — high prio → large weight
    //   delta_weighted = delta * weight / 1024
    //   vruntime += delta_weighted        — high prio → slow vruntime growth
    // The `prio + 1` avoids div-by-zero; the 1024 base matches Linux's
    // NICE_0_LOAD. See the W36 header comment above for the full table.
    let prio = pt_get_prio(tbl, task_idx) as u32;
    let weight = 1024 / (prio + 1);
    let delta_weighted = delta * (weight as u64) / 1024;
    let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, task_idx);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, task_idx, v + delta_weighted);
    // Step 2: preemption check (W11S2 — unchanged). If the runqueue is
    // empty there is nothing to preempt to — leave need_resched alone.
    if rq.head == 256 {
        return;
    }
    // Walk the runqueue linked list to find the minimum vruntime among
    // the READY tasks. Same walk pattern as sched_dequeue's min-vruntime
    // search, but without the prev-tracking (we don't remove anything).
    let min_vruntime = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, rq.head);
    let cur = pt_get_next(tbl, rq.head);
    while cur != 256 {
        let v2 = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, cur as u32);
        if v2 < min_vruntime {
            min_vruntime = v2;
        }
        cur = pt_get_next(tbl, cur as u32);
    }
    // Compare current's (post-bump) vruntime to the min. Guard the
    // subtraction so u64 underflow can't make an unfair task look fair.
    let cur_vruntime = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, task_idx);
    if cur_vruntime > min_vruntime {
        let diff = cur_vruntime - min_vruntime;
        if diff > 1000000 {
            rq.need_resched = 1;
        }
    }
}

// sched_check_resched: return the current need_resched flag. Called by
// the timer-IRQ handler (R4c) after sched_tick to decide whether to
// invoke schedule(). Returns 1 if a preemption is requested, 0
// otherwise. Pure State<T> field read — no pointer syntax.
//
// State<T> params: 1 (rq — read only).
fn sched_check_resched(rq: State<Runqueue>) -> u32 {
    return rq.need_resched;
}

// sched_clear_resched: clear the need_resched flag. Called by the
// timer-IRQ handler (or whichever caller serviced the preemption
// request) after it has either called schedule() OR decided to ignore
// the request (e.g. preempt_count != 0 — see womb/kernel/smp/percpu.vuma
// percpu_preemptable). Resets the flag so subsequent ticks start from a
// clean slate — without this, a single preemption request would persist
// forever and every subsequent tick would re-trigger schedule().
//
// State<T> params: 1 (rq — write only).
fn sched_clear_resched(rq: State<Runqueue>) {
    rq.need_resched = 0;
}

// sched_yield: re-enqueue the current task (add it to the tail of the
// runqueue) and pick the next task to run (sched_dequeue). Returns the
// next task's index (or 256 if the runqueue is now empty — but that
// can't happen if `current` was just enqueued).
fn sched_yield(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, current: u32) -> u32 {
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, current);
    return sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// W36 additions: sys_sched_yield, sys_nice, sched_dump.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Three new functions that expose scheduler operations as syscall-style
// wrappers (matching the convention of sys_waitpid in K4e's
// womb/kernel/proc/wait.vuma and sys_socket in K9b's net/socket.vuma):
//
//   * sys_sched_yield(tbl, percpu) — cooperative yield. Reads the current
//     task from PerCpu, marks it READY, picks the min-vruntime READY task
//     from the ProcessTable (CFS pick), marks it RUNNING, updates
//     percpu.current_task, returns its index. Wraps the existing
//     sched_yield(rq, tbl, current) for the case where the caller has the
//     PerCpu block but not the Runqueue (e.g. a syscall trampoline that
//     only knows "this CPU's current task").
//
//   * sys_nice(tbl, task_idx, inc) — adjust a task's priority by `inc`
//     (POSIX nice(2) semantics). Clamps the result to 0..139 (Linux's
//     valid prio range: 0..99 = realtime, 100..139 = nice -20..+19). A
//     negative `inc` raises priority (lower prio value = higher priority
//     in the CFS weight formula `weight = 1024 / (prio + 1)`).
//
//   * sched_dump(tbl) — diagnostic dump. Walks all 256 Task slots, and
//     for each non-FREE slot (state != 0) writes a line to fd 1 with the
//     task's PID, STATE, PRIO, and VRUNTIME. Used by the kernel shell's
//     `ps` command (W14+) and by post-mortem debug (dump the table on
//     panic). Output uses string-literal labels ("PID=", " STATE=", etc.)
//     cast to Address via the same PMT-pure `s as Address` pattern that
//     K12a's panic.vuma uses for its banner.

// sys_sched_yield: cooperative yield via the PerCpu current_task pointer.
//
//   tbl:     caller-allocated State<ProcessTable> (modified — task states
//            are flipped READY↔RUNNING).
//   percpu:  caller-allocated State<PerCpu> (modified — current_task is
//            updated to the picked next task).
//
// Behavior:
//   1. current = percpu.current_task  — read the calling CPU's running task.
//   2. pt_set_state(tbl, current, 2)  — "enqueue self": mark current READY
//      (runnable, eligible for selection). This is the table-walk
//      equivalent of sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, current) — without the rq
//      linked-list bookkeeping (sys_sched_yield doesn't take an rq param,
//      so it can't thread current onto the rq list; instead it relies on
//      the table-walk pick in step 3 finding current as a candidate).
//   3. Walk all 256 Task slots, find the min-vruntime task with state==2
//      (READY). Initialize `next = current` and `min_v = vruntime(current)`
//      so that if NO other READY task has a lower vruntime, current is
//      re-picked (a no-op yield — the task keeps running, which is the
//      correct CFS behavior when current is still the most-fair candidate).
//   4. pt_set_state(tbl, next, 1)     — "dequeue next": mark the picked
//      task RUNNING.
//   5. percpu.current_task = next     — update the PerCpu current_task.
//   6. return next.
//
// State<T> params: 2 (tbl + percpu), per the K4b contract's "1-2 per
// function" rule. The function does NOT take an rq parameter — it walks
// the ProcessTable directly (O(n) over 256 slots) rather than the rq
// linked list (O(n) over rq.count slots). The table-walk is slower than
// the linked-list walk, but it avoids the rq parameter and matches the
// sys_waitpid(tbl, ...) / sys_socket(tbl, ...) convention of "sys_*
// wrappers take the table + a per-CPU/context pointer." For the hot path
// (timer-driven preemption), use sched_yield(rq, tbl, current) directly.
//
// PMT discipline: no `*ptr`/`&x`/`allocate`/`free`. Pure State<T> field
// access (tbl.states[i], percpu.current_task, pt_get_vruntime,
// pt_set_state). The function returns u32 (the picked task index).
fn sys_sched_yield(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, percpu: State<PerCpu>) -> u32 {
    let current = percpu.current_task;
    // Enqueue self: mark current as READY (runnable).
    pt_set_state(tbl, current, 2);
    // Dequeue next: CFS pick — min vruntime among READY tasks.
    // Initialize with `current` so a no-op yield (current is still the
    // most-fair candidate) re-picks current rather than returning 256.
    let next = current;
    let min_v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, current);
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        let s = pt_get_state(tbl, i);
        if s == 2 {
            let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, i);
            if v < min_v {
                min_v = v;
                next = i;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // Mark the picked task RUNNING and update PerCpu.
    pt_set_state(tbl, next, 1);
    percpu.current_task = next;
    return next;
}

// sys_nice: adjust a task's priority by `inc` (POSIX nice(2)).
//
//   tbl:      caller-allocated State<ProcessTable> (modified — prio is
//             updated).
//   task_idx: the Task slot whose prio is being adjusted.
//   inc:      signed increment. Positive = lower priority (higher prio
//             value = smaller CFS weight = faster vruntime growth = less
//             CPU). Negative = raise priority (requires CAP_SYS_NICE in
//             a real kernel — W36 does no permission check; K13+ may add
//             one via the credentials subsystem).
//
// Behavior:
//   1. Read current prio.
//   2. np = prio + inc  (signed arithmetic in i32 — prio is u8 but the
//      sum can go negative or above 255, so we widen to i32 first).
//   3. Clamp np to 0..139 (Linux's valid prio range). The lower bound 0
//      is the highest priority (prio 0 → weight 1024 → no vruntime decay).
//      The upper bound 139 is the lowest priority (prio 139 → weight 7 →
//      maximum vruntime decay). Values 0..99 correspond to realtime
//      priorities (SCHED_FIFO/SCHED_RR in Linux); 100..139 correspond to
//      nice -20..+19 (SCHED_NORMAL/CFS).
//   4. Write clamped prio back.
//
// State<T> params: 1 (tbl). The `inc` is i32 (signed) to allow negative
// increments. The arithmetic is done in i32 locals (prio widened via
// `as i32`, np computed as i32 + i32). The final write truncates back to
// u8 via `np as u8` — safe because the clamp guarantees 0 <= np <= 139,
// which fits in u8.
//
// PMT discipline: no `*ptr`/`&x`/`allocate`/`free`. Pure State<T> field
// access via pt_get_prio / pt_set_prio.
fn sys_nice(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, inc: i32) {
    let cur = pt_get_prio(tbl, task_idx) as i32;
    let np = cur + inc;
    if np < 0 {
        pt_set_prio(tbl, task_idx, 0);
    } else {
        if np > 139 {
            pt_set_prio(tbl, task_idx, 139);
        } else {
            pt_set_prio(tbl, task_idx, np as u8);
        }
    }
}

// sched_write_u64: write `n` to fd 1 as decimal ASCII. Helper for
// sched_dump (not part of the public scheduler surface — file-local).
//
// Behavior:
//   * n == 0: write the single byte '0' (48 ASCII).
//   * n > 0:  extract digits LSB-first into a SchedBuf (val % 10 + 48),
//             reverse them in-place (so the buffer holds MSB-first), then
//             write `pos` bytes to fd 1.
//
// The SchedBuf is allocated fresh per call via state_new(SchedBuf) —
// cheap (arena-bump-pointer, no malloc), and avoids any cross-call state.
// 20 bytes is enough for the longest u64 decimal (18446744073709551615);
// SchedBuf.data is 32 bytes (headroom).
//
// PMT discipline: no `*ptr`/`&x`/`allocate`/`free`. The `buf as Address`
// cast is the pure-arena-address extraction documented in K12b's kmsg.vuma
// ("The `out as Address` cast is a pure-arena-address extraction").
//
// State<T> locals: 1 (buf). No State<T> params (the function takes only
// the u64 `n` — it doesn't read or write any persistent state).
fn sched_write_u64(n: u64) {
    let buf = state_new(SchedBuf);
    if n == 0 {
        buf.data[0] = 48;  // '0'
        let addr = buf as Address;
        write(1, addr, 1);
        return;
    }
    // Extract digits LSB-first into buf.data[0..pos).
    let pos = 0;
    let val = n;
    while val > 0 {
        let d = val % 10;
        buf.data[pos] = (d + 48) as u8;
        pos = pos + 1;
        val = val / 10;
    }
    // Reverse in-place so buf.data[0..pos) holds MSB-first decimal.
    let i = 0;
    let j = pos;
    while i < j {
        j = j - 1;
        let tmp = buf.data[i];
        buf.data[i] = buf.data[j];
        buf.data[j] = tmp;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let addr = buf as Address;
    write(1, addr, pos as i64);
}

// sched_dump: diagnostic dump of all non-FREE Task slots to fd 1.
//
//   tbl: caller-allocated State<ProcessTable> (read-only — not modified).
//
// For each slot i in 0..255 where pt_get_state(tbl, i) != 0 (non-FREE),
// writes a single line to fd 1 in the format:
//     PID=<pid> STATE=<state> PRIO=<prio> VRUNTIME=<vruntime>\n
// where <pid> is the u32 PID (decimal), <state> is the u8 state (1=
// RUNNING, 2=READY, etc.), <prio> is the u8 prio (0..139), and
// <vruntime> is the u64 vruntime (decimal nanoseconds).
//
// The labels are emitted as string-literal addresses (`"PID=" as Address`)
// — the VUMA codegen places the literal in the data section and yields
// its address via the `as Address` cast. This is the PMT-pure equivalent
// of C's `write(1, "PID=", 4)` — no printf, no varargs, no heap. Same
// pattern as K12a's panic.vuma banner (which builds the banner
// byte-by-byte in a SchedBuf because panic.vuma predates the
// `"..." as Address` codegen path; W36 uses the string-literal-as-Address
// form directly because it's shorter and the codegen supports it).
//
// Output is observable but NOT verified by the self-test (verifying
// stdout would require capturing fd 1, which needs K9a's socket layer or
// a fork+pipe dance — out of scope for W36's hosted self-test, same
// exclusion as K12b's dmesg in kmsg.vuma).
//
// State<T> params: 1 (tbl — read only). State<T> locals: 1 per
// sched_write_u64 call (the SchedBuf). No `*ptr`/`&x`/`allocate`/`free`.
fn sched_dump(tbl: State<ProcessTable>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        let s = pt_get_state(tbl, i);
        if s != 0 {
            // "PID=" (4 bytes) + pid decimal.
            let pid_label = "PID=" as Address;
            write(1, pid_label, 4);
            sched_write_u64(pt_get_pid(tbl, i) as u64);
            // " STATE=" (7 bytes: space + STATE + =) + state decimal.
            let state_label = " STATE=" as Address;
            write(1, state_label, 7);
            sched_write_u64(s as u64);
            // " PRIO=" (6 bytes: space + PRIO + =) + prio decimal.
            let prio_label = " PRIO=" as Address;
            write(1, prio_label, 6);
            sched_write_u64(pt_get_prio(tbl, i) as u64);
            // " VRUNTIME=" (10 bytes: space + VRUNTIME + =) + vruntime decimal.
            let vr_label = " VRUNTIME=" as Address;
            write(1, vr_label, 10);
            sched_write_u64(pt_get_vruntime(tbl, i));
            // "\n" (1 byte) — line terminator.
            let nl = "\n" as Address;
            write(1, nl, 1);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Context save/restore (NEW in R4a+b).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// These two functions are the R4a+b additions to the scheduler surface.
// They copy tf.{pc,sp,status} ↔ tbl.{mm_root,fs_root,fds} for the Task
// slot `task_idx`. The caller (kmain / R4c syscall dispatcher / timer
// trampoline) calls them around schedule() to save the outgoing task's
// TrapFrame and load the incoming task's TrapFrame. The actual CPU
// register load (writing the TrapFrame's GPRs into the hardware regs)
// is the per-arch arch_context_switch's job, declared in the 3 switch.vuma
// files.
//
// The contract specifies that the scheduler use ONLY the superset
// TrapFrame's scalar fields (pc, sp, status) plus the gpr helper accessors
// (tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr). NO arch-specific fields (tf.rip, tf.x0, etc.)
// exist on the superset TrapFrame — R2a deliberately collapsed all 3
// arches' register files into the gpr [u8; 256] array indexed by canonical
// slot (see R2a's trap_frame.vuma header for the slot assignment per arch).

// sched_save_context: copy tf.{pc, sp, status} into the Task slot's
// saved-context overload fields (mm_root, fs_root, fds). See the file
// header "Saved-context overload" section for the field mapping and the
// safety rationale (the mm/fs/fds fields are not dereferenced during the
// context-switch window, so overloading them as saved-PC/SP/status is
// safe).
//
// State<T> params: 2 (tbl + tf). Pure field-copies (3 pt_set_* calls,
// each reading 1 scalar u64 from tf and writing it via 8-byte LE pack
// into the parallel byte array). No `*ptr`/`&tf`/`allocate` anywhere.
fn sched_save_context(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    pt_set_mm_root(tbl, task_idx, tf.pc);      // saved PC → mm_root
    pt_set_fs_root(tbl, task_idx, tf.sp);      // saved SP → fs_root
    pt_set_fds(tbl, task_idx, tf.status);      // saved status → fds
}

// sched_restore_context: copy the Task slot's saved-context overload
// fields (mm_root, fs_root, fds) back into tf.{pc, sp, status}. The
// inverse of sched_save_context — same 3 field mappings, reversed
// direction.
//
// State<T> params: 2 (tbl + tf). Pure field-copies (3 pt_get_* calls,
// each reading 8-byte LE unpack from the parallel byte array and writing
// it as a scalar u64 into tf).
fn sched_restore_context(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    tf.pc = pt_get_mm_root(tbl, task_idx);     // mm_root → saved PC
    tf.sp = pt_get_fs_root(tbl, task_idx);     // fs_root → saved SP
    tf.status = pt_get_fds(tbl, task_idx);     // fds → saved status
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the R4a+b DoD + W11S2 preemption extension).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a Runqueue, a ProcessTable, and a TrapFrame via state_new.
// Init the runqueue (sched_init) and the TrapFrame (tf_init). Manually
// set up 2 tasks with different vruntimes (Task 0: vruntime=100, Task 1:
// vruntime=50), enqueue both, then:
//   (1) rq.count == 2 (both tasks enqueued).
//   (2) schedule() returns 1 (Task 1, vruntime=50, the min).
//   (3) sched_save_context(tbl, 1, tf) with tf.pc = 4096, then
//       sched_restore_context(tbl, 1, tf) on a fresh TrapFrame yields
//       tf.pc == 4096 — round-trip through the saved-context overload.
// W11S2 preemption extension (Task 1 is RUNNING, Task 0 is the only
// READY task in the runqueue with vruntime=100):
//   (4) rq.need_resched == 0 right after sched_init (clean slate).
//   (5) sched_tick with a SMALL delta (100) — Task 1 vruntime goes
//       50→150, min in runqueue is 100, diff=50 < PREEMPT_THRESHOLD
//       (1000000) — need_resched stays 0.
//   (6) sched_tick with a LARGE delta (2000000) — Task 1 vruntime goes
//       150→2000150, min=100, diff=2000050 > threshold — need_resched
//       flips to 1.
//   (7) sched_check_resched(rq) returns 1 (flag is observable).
//   (8) sched_clear_resched(rq) clears it.
//   (9) sched_check_resched(rq) returns 0 (clear is observable).
// Returns 0 on success, 1..9 on the corresponding check failure.
//
// Per the contract's "decimal constants" rule: all non-zero constants
// use DECIMAL (100, 50, 4096, 2000000, 1000000) — decimal avoids the
// parser's hex-literal path (see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header
// for the rationale). (4096 = 0x1000, 100 and 50 are arbitrary test
// vruntimes, 2000000 ns = 2 ms — a typical tick interval scaled up to
// exceed the 1 ms threshold in one tick for test speed.)

fn main() -> i32 {
    let rq = state_new(Runqueue);
    let tbl = state_new(ProcessTable);
    let tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    let percpu = state_new(PerCpu);
    sched_init(rq);
    tf_init(tf);
    // Set up 2 tasks with different vruntimes.
    // Task 0: vruntime=100, Task 1: vruntime=50.
    pt_set_state(tbl, 0, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 0, 100);
    pt_set_state(tbl, 1, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 1, 50);
    // Enqueue both.
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, 0);
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, 1);
    if rq.count != 2 { return 1; }
    // schedule() should return Task 1 (vruntime=50, the min).
    let next = schedule(rq, tbl);
    if next != 1 { return 2; }
    // Round-trip tf.pc through sched_save_context + sched_restore_context.
    tf.pc = 4096;
    tf.sp = 8192;
    tf.status = 256;
    sched_save_context(tbl, 1, tf);
    // Clear tf to prove restore actually writes back.
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    sched_restore_context(tbl, 1, tf);
    if tf.pc != 4096 { return 3; }
    // ── W11S2 preemption checks ─────────────────────────────────────────
    // After schedule() above, Task 1 is RUNNING (dequeued), Task 0 is
    // the only READY task in the runqueue (vruntime=100).
    // (4) need_resched must start at 0 (sched_init zeroed it).
    if rq.need_resched != 0 { return 4; }
    // (5) Small tick — diff stays below threshold, no preemption.
    sched_tick(rq, tbl, 1, 100);
    if rq.need_resched != 0 { return 5; }
    // (6) Large tick — diff exceeds threshold, need_resched flips to 1.
    //     Task 1 vruntime: 150 → 2000150. Min in rq: 100. Diff = 2000050.
    sched_tick(rq, tbl, 1, 2000000);
    if rq.need_resched != 1 { return 6; }
    // (7) sched_check_resched observes the flag.
    if sched_check_resched(rq) != 1 { return 7; }
    // (8) sched_clear_resched clears it.
    sched_clear_resched(rq);
    if rq.need_resched != 0 { return 8; }
    // (9) sched_check_resched observes the cleared flag.
    if sched_check_resched(rq) != 0 { return 9; }
    // ── W36 priority-weighted vruntime ──────────────────────────────────
    // Task 2: prio=7, vruntime=0. sched_tick with delta=1024.
    //   weight = 1024 / (7 + 1) = 128
    //   delta_weighted = 1024 * 128 / 1024 = 128
    //   vruntime: 0 → 128
    // (10) Verify the priority-weighted vruntime increment.
    pt_set_state(tbl, 2, 1);
    pt_set_prio(tbl, 2, 7);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 2, 0);
    sched_tick(rq, tbl, 2, 1024);
    if pt_get_vruntime(tbl, 2) != 128 { return 10; }
    // ── W36 sys_nice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // (11) sys_nice(tbl, 2, 5): prio 7 → 12.
    sys_nice(tbl, 2, 5);
    if pt_get_prio(tbl, 2) != 12 { return 11; }
    // (12) sys_nice(tbl, 2, 200): prio 12 → clamp to 139.
    sys_nice(tbl, 2, 200);
    if pt_get_prio(tbl, 2) != 139 { return 12; }
    // (13) sys_nice(tbl, 2, -200): prio 139 → clamp to 0.
    //   (0 - 200 avoids the parser's negative-literal path — same
    //    convention as wait.vuma's `0 - 1` for -EAGAIN.)
    sys_nice(tbl, 2, 0 - 200);
    if pt_get_prio(tbl, 2) != 0 { return 13; }
    // ── W36 sys_sched_yield ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Task 3: READY, vruntime=10 (lower — should be picked).
    // Task 4: RUNNING, vruntime=20 (current).
    // percpu.current_task = 4. sys_sched_yield should pick Task 3.
    // (14) Verify sys_sched_yield returns 3 (min-vruntime READY task).
    pt_set_state(tbl, 3, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 3, 10);
    pt_set_state(tbl, 4, 1);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 4, 20);
    percpu.current_task = 4;
    let yielded = sys_sched_yield(tbl, percpu);
    if yielded != 3 { return 14; }
    // (15) percpu.current_task updated to 3.
    if percpu.current_task != 3 { return 15; }
    // (16) Task 4 is now READY (was RUNNING, yielded).
    if pt_get_state(tbl, 4) != 2 { return 16; }
    // (17) Task 3 is now RUNNING (was READY, picked).
    if pt_get_state(tbl, 3) != 1 { return 17; }
    // ── W36 sched_dump (observable, not verified — same exclusion as ──
    // ── K12b's dmesg: capturing fd 1 needs K9a's socket layer).       ──
    // (18) sched_dump runs without crashing on a table with 5 non-FREE
    //     tasks (Tasks 0..4). The output goes to stdout; we only verify
    //     the function returns (no return value to check — it's `-> ()`).
    sched_dump(tbl);
    return 0;
}
